          "title": "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "440aa969-1052-5c1b-b16c-6b14fda6479b",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "The labeled lines are level curves of a function $f(x,y)$.\n\n![Labeled level curves](/tmp/ca9837-templates-z7ii9jxi/17592186179712/plot-d4a950973241bec71e73387c8fbe459a02e67f973e9e4ad56a796b167138eecb.png)\n\nRecall that gradient vectors are **perpendicular** to level curves and point toward **increasing** function values. Which diagram could show its gradient vector field? Arrows show directions only.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The level curves are parallel straight lines. A normal is proportional to $(-2,-1)$. Use the labels to choose its sense: moving in that direction raises the labels, so it is the gradient direction. Tangent arrows fail perpendicularity; the opposite normal points toward smaller labels.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](/tmp/ca9837-templates-z7ii9jxi/17592186179712/plot-715eb491c11ef0bf1ffa9472daed9551ddf0d6a2475a02e61eb16e32534f0786.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](/tmp/ca9837-templates-z7ii9jxi/17592186179712/plot-24ac217abcc0298295db76c3df006af6890d58fbbe0c1efba64d3f3d358e769b.png)",
