    {
      "level": "hard",
      "template_selector": null,
      "record": {
        "knowledge_point": {
          "db_id": 17592186179712,
          "id": "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b",
          "title": "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "28a5cfa6-7561-5de8-abfb-333343e32d88",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=-y^2+\\left(x+1\\right)$? Use the relationship between level curves and gradient directions. Arrows show directions only.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First set $f(x,y)=c$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=c\\\\-y^2+\\left(x+1\\right)&=c\\\\x&=-1+c+y^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese level curves are right-opening parabolas. With $y$ fixed, moving right increases the function. With $x$ fixed, moving toward $y=0$ increases it.\n\n![Derived labeled level curves](/tmp/ca9837-templates-z7ii9jxi/17592186179712/plot-90b281f3aab3c51ae71060bb4850317326e48fcbf7ddd86279bbf9b3e84b30ea.png)\n\nA derivative check gives $\\nabla f=\\left\\langle 1,-2y\\right\\rangle$. These directions are normal to the level curves and point toward increasing values. Other curve shapes have a different normal direction somewhere; a reversed field, if offered, points downhill.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](/tmp/ca9837-templates-z7ii9jxi/17592186179712/plot-b496284915b289f22169cf7e265ee540d8b653a322b56e64b917c6156f61a191.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](/tmp/ca9837-templates-z7ii9jxi/17592186179712/plot-e8937b50f5808d61b4c01ae2ef451476dc11d30f64446a5baa9e910e04ca8b7e.png)",
