    {
      "level": "moderate",
      "template_selector": null,
      "record": {
        "knowledge_point": {
          "db_id": 17592186179712,
          "id": "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b",
          "title": "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "01d541d1-f5de-5560-992b-669c14d88e9b",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
          ":question/problem": "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given gradient directions](/tmp/ca9837-templates-z7ii9jxi/17592186179712/plot-1204b4d6fd7f54049b0fda8d45ade5d5ba18cd629338ee1aa4c9ab1dc37a4450.png)",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The field must be perpendicular to every regular level curve. One compatible function, used here to check the geometric match, is $f(x,y)=x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=c\\\\x&=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese level curves are parallel straight lines. The labels increase in the normal direction proportional to $(1,0)$. A perpendicular field pointing the opposite way would decrease the function. Unlabeled contours do not specify which side has larger values; the required test for these choices is perpendicularity, including at off-axis points.\n\nA derivative check gives $\\nabla f=\\left\\langle 1,0\\right\\rangle$. These directions are normal to the level curves and point toward increasing values. Other curve shapes have a different normal direction somewhere; a reversed field, if offered, points downhill.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](/tmp/ca9837-templates-z7ii9jxi/17592186179712/plot-3d182172906cc4a5cf5f2e3569ca957664e6295346fec7050bf4f3e1ab8c6184.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](/tmp/ca9837-templates-z7ii9jxi/17592186179712/plot-243cc9efa18c844d99637d1c275bf69cadd7e3efb9c67e7235b04baa5e6aceb6.png)",
